# Pixel-Art Game Engine Specification (Phaser 3 + JavaScript)

You are building a **pure, content-agnostic 2D pixel-art game engine** on top of
Phaser 3. The engine must know nothing about any specific game's story, theme,
or assets. Every visual asset, character, background, and UI screen is
described by **JSON manifests** that the engine reads and renders generically.
The goal: a developer should be able to add a brand-new character, background,
or screen mode by dropping files into the correct folder and writing a JSON
file — **without touching engine code**.

Build this as a modular JavaScript codebase (ES modules), using Phaser 3's
Arcade physics, Scene system, and Texture/Animation managers as the
rendering backbone. Below is the full functional specification, organized by
subsystem. Implement each subsystem as an independent module with a clean,
documented public API (JSDoc on every exported function/class).

---

## 1. Directory & Asset Convention

All content lives under a predictable, convention-based tree so the engine
can auto-discover assets without hardcoded paths per asset:

```
/graphics
  /sprites
    /main_char
      manifest.json
      /idle/<direction>.png
      /animations/<state>/<direction>/frame_###.png
    /npc_<name>/
      manifest.json
      ...
  /backgrounds
    /<background_id>/
      manifest.json
      /layers/<layer_name>.png   (parallax layers, bottom-to-top order)
      /tiles/<tileset>.png        (optional tileset sheets)
  /ui
    /windows/<window_skin>/
      manifest.json
      9-slice panel textures, button states, fonts
  /fx
    particles, overlays, shaders (CRT, glitch, scanlines)

/screens
  /<screen_id>/
    manifest.json      (declares which systems/UI this screen uses)

/audio
  /music
  /sfx

/config
  settings.default.json
  controls.default.json
  engine.config.json
```

The engine must provide an **AssetRegistry** module that, given a root path,
scans manifests recursively, validates them against a JSON Schema (see
section 2), and builds an in-memory index of every sprite, background,
screen, and UI skin available — before Phaser's preload phase begins. Invalid
or missing manifests must fail loudly with a descriptive console error naming
the file and the missing/invalid field, never fail silently.

