## 4. Modular Screen / Mode System ("World" vs "Terminal" vs anything else)

This is the most important architectural piece: the game is composed of
**interchangeable "modes"** (full-screen contexts) — e.g. a top-down
world-exploration mode and a completely different terminal/CLI mode — and the
engine must treat them as **plug-in modules**, not two hardcoded Phaser
scenes with ad-hoc switching logic.

### 4.1 Screen manifest

```jsonc
{
  "id": "world",
  "display_name": "World Exploration",
  "controller": "WorldScreenController",   // registered JS class, see 4.2
  "input_profile": "movement",              // which input bindings are active
  "background": "school_hallway",            // default background id, optional
  "ui_windows": ["hud_fragments"],           // UI windows auto-opened on enter
  "transition_in": "fade",
  "transition_out": "fade"
}
```

```jsonc
{
  "id": "terminal",
  "display_name": "Fake Kali Terminal",
  "controller": "TerminalScreenController",
  "input_profile": "text-entry",
  "background": null,
  "ui_windows": ["terminal_crt_overlay"],
  "transition_in": "crt-flicker",
  "transition_out": "crt-flicker"
}
```

### 4.2 Screen controller contract

Every screen mode is implemented as a class conforming to a fixed interface
(document this as an abstract base class or a documented duck-typed
contract):

```js
class ScreenController {
  constructor(engine, manifest) {}
  onEnter(params) {}      // called when ScreenManager activates this mode
  onExit() {}             // called before this mode is torn down
  onUpdate(time, delta) {} // per-frame tick while active
  onInput(action, event) {} // routed input events, see 4.3
  onPause() {}             // called if another screen is pushed on top (modal)
  onResume() {}            // called when this screen regains focus
}
```

- A **`ScreenManager`** maintains a **stack** of active screens (not just a
  single "current scene"), so a settings window or a dialog box can be
  **pushed on top of** the World or Terminal screen as a modal without
  destroying the underlying one (e.g., pause menu over gameplay). `push(id,
  params)`, `pop()`, `switchTo(id, params)` (clears stack and replaces top
  level), and `replaceTop(id, params)` must all be supported.
- Switching screens must run through a **transition pipeline**: each
  manifest declares `transition_in`/`transition_out` by name (`"fade"`,
  `"crt-flicker"`, `"instant"`, `"wipe-left"`, …), and the engine resolves
  these names to a registry of transition effect functions — so adding a new
  transition style is adding one function to a registry, not editing
  `ScreenManager`.
- `ScreenManager` must be entirely agnostic about what a "world" or
  "terminal" screen actually *is*. It only knows: activate controller, call
  lifecycle hooks, manage the stack, run transitions. All world-specific or
  terminal-specific logic lives inside that mode's own controller class and
  nowhere else.
- New screen modes (e.g., a future "minimap", "dialogue", "inventory" full
  screen) must be addable by (a) writing a new controller class implementing
  the contract, (b) registering it by name in a `ScreenControllerRegistry`,
  and (c) adding a manifest JSON. No existing screen's code should require
  edits.

### 4.3 Input routing per mode

- An **`InputRouter`** maps raw keyboard/gamepad events to abstract **named
  actions** (e.g., `move-up`, `interact`, `confirm`, `cancel`,
  `text-char`, `text-backspace`) via a swappable **input profile** JSON
  (`/config/controls.default.json`), not hardcoded key codes scattered
  through scene code.
- Each screen manifest declares which `input_profile` is active while it has
  focus (`"movement"` for World, `"text-entry"` for Terminal, `"menu-nav"`
  for a settings window). `ScreenManager` must swap the active input profile
  automatically on `onEnter`/`onExit`, and must route input **only to the
  top of the screen stack** (so a modal settings window intercepts input and
  the World underneath does not also react to the same keypress).
- Input profiles and key bindings must be **user-remappable** at runtime
  (see section 6) without engine code changes — rebinding writes to the same
  JSON structure the engine reads at boot.

