## 5. Modular UI Window System (used by Settings, Terminal overlay, Dialogs, HUD, etc.)

Build **one generic window/panel system** reused by every UI surface in the
game — settings menu, terminal CRT frame, HUD fragment counter, dialogue
boxes, pause menu — rather than one-off UI code per feature.

### 5.1 Window manifest / skin

```jsonc
{
  "id": "settings_window",
  "skin": "default_panel",            // references /graphics/ui/windows/<skin>/
  "layout": "vertical-list",           // "vertical-list" | "grid" | "free" | "form"
  "size": { "width": 280, "height": 200 },
  "anchor": "center",
  "closable": true,
  "modal": true,
  "elements": [
    { "type": "label", "text": "SETTINGS" },
    { "type": "slider", "id": "music_volume", "label": "Music", "min": 0, "max": 1, "bind": "settings.audio.music" },
    { "type": "slider", "id": "sfx_volume", "label": "SFX", "min": 0, "max": 1, "bind": "settings.audio.sfx" },
    { "type": "toggle", "id": "glitch_fx", "label": "Glitch FX", "bind": "settings.video.glitchEnabled" },
    { "type": "keybind_list", "bind": "controls" },
    { "type": "button", "label": "Back", "action": "close-window" }
  ]
}
```

Requirements:

- A **`WindowFactory`** builds a fully interactive Phaser UI container from
  any window manifest: panel background (9-slice scalable to any
  `size`), and a declared list of **UI element types** (`label`, `button`,
  `slider`, `toggle`, `text-input`, `keybind_list`, `scrollable-text`,
  `list`). Every element type is implemented once, generically, and reused
  everywhere (e.g., the Terminal's scrollable output uses the same
  `scrollable-text` element as a dialogue box would).
- Windows bind their values to a central **`SettingsStore`** (section 6) via
  the `bind` path declared in the manifest — moving a slider immediately
  updates the store and persists it; nothing in `WindowFactory` needs to
  know what "music volume" means semantically.
- Windows support **declarative actions** (`"action": "close-window"`,
  `"action": "open-window:credits"`, `"action": "emit:crack-attempt"`) so
  designers can wire up buttons without writing JS for each one — the engine
  provides an `ActionRegistry` mapping action strings to handler functions,
  extensible by gameplay code.
- The **Terminal mode's CRT frame** and the **Settings window** must both be
  built from this same window system (different skins/manifests), proving
  the system is truly mode-agnostic — not "settings UI" and "terminal UI" as
  separate implementations.
- Support nested/child windows (a confirm dialog opened from inside the
  settings window) via the same `ScreenManager` push/pop modal stack used
  for full screens (section 4.2) — a "window" is just a lightweight modal
  screen under the hood, reusing the same stack and transition machinery.

