## 6. Settings, Persistence & Config

- A single **`SettingsStore`** (plain reactive object + pub/sub) holds all
  runtime-configurable values: audio volumes, control bindings, video
  toggles (pixel-perfect scaling on/off, glitch FX on/off, CRT overlay
  on/off), accessibility options (text size, reduced flashing/shake for the
  Glitch system in section 7).
- Must load defaults from `/config/settings.default.json`, then overlay any
  saved values from `localStorage` (namespaced key, versioned schema so old
  saves degrade gracefully if fields are added later).
- Any module reading a setting (e.g., `GlitchManager` reading
  `settings.video.glitchEnabled`, or `AudioManager` reading
  `settings.audio.sfx`) must subscribe to changes rather than read once at
  boot, so toggling a setting in the modal Settings window takes effect
  immediately without a restart.
- Controls rebinding (section 4.3) reads/writes through this same store
  under a `controls` namespace.

---

## 7. Generic Visual FX Layer (glitch, CRT, tint, shake — reusable across modes)

- An **`FxManager`** exposes mode-agnostic primitives: `shake(intensity,
  duration)`, `flash(color, duration)`, `applyTint(color, alpha)`,
  `crtOverlay(enabled)`, `scanlines(enabled)`, `chromaticAberration(amount)`.
- These primitives must be callable from **any** screen controller (World's
  glitch-on-fragment-progress, Terminal's CRT boot-up flicker) — i.e., FX are
  not owned by one scene; they operate on whichever camera/container the
  calling screen passes in.
- Respect the accessibility settings from section 6 (e.g., if
  "reduced motion/flashing" is on, `shake`/`flash` intensities are
  automatically scaled down or disabled).

---

## 8. Rendering & Scaling Baseline

- Pixel-perfect rendering: `pixelArt: true` in Phaser config, nearest-neighbor
  texture filtering enforced globally, no exceptions per-sprite.
- Integer-only camera/scale-manager zoom levels to avoid pixel shimmer;
  provide a `DisplayManager` that computes the best integer scale for the
  current window size and exposes `setScale(n)` for a settings-controlled
  zoom option.
- All coordinates for gameplay logic operate in a fixed internal resolution
  (engine-config value, e.g. 480×270); the `DisplayManager` handles scaling
  that up to the actual canvas/window size via Phaser's ScaleManager —
  gameplay code must never read `window.innerWidth` directly.

---

## 9. Non-Functional / Engineering Requirements

- **Zero content coupling**: grep-test — no file under `/engine` (core
  systems) may contain a string literal referencing a specific character
  name, background name, screen id, or window id. Those only ever appear in
  JSON manifests or in the top-level game-specific bootstrap file that wires
  manifests to the engine at startup.
- **Fail loud, fail early**: any manifest validation error must throw/log
  with the exact file path and field at load time (ideally with a dev-mode
  on-screen error overlay), never silently fall back in a way that hides a
  content bug.
- **Full JSDoc** on every public class/function (params, return type, thrown
  errors) so that an external document generator (or another AI agent)
  could produce accurate API docs without reading implementation bodies.
- **Pure functions where possible**: direction resolution, animation key
  naming, and settings-path resolution should be implemented as pure,
  unit-testable utility functions, separate from Phaser-coupled classes.
- Favor **composition over inheritance**: `Actor` composes a
  `SpriteDefinition` + `AnimationFactory` output rather than subclassing per
  character; `ScreenController` implementations compose `BackgroundLoader`,
  `WindowFactory`, `InputRouter` rather than reimplementing any of them.

---

## 10. Deliverable Expectations

Produce, as a modular codebase matching the above:

1. `engine/` — all generic systems described above (AssetRegistry,
   SpriteDefinition/AnimationFactory/DirectionResolver, BackgroundLoader,
   ScreenManager/ScreenControllerRegistry, InputRouter, WindowFactory/
   ActionRegistry, SettingsStore, FxManager, DisplayManager).
2. A minimal **example content pack** (one character manifest, one
   background, two screen modes — "world" and "terminal" — one settings
   window) that proves every system end-to-end, clearly separated from
   `engine/` so it's obvious what is reusable engine code versus
   example/game-specific content.
3. Markdown documentation (`README.md`, `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `API.md`) at the
   same level of rigor as the spec above, written for consumption by another
   AI coding agent with no other context.
